# 规则概述
该规则要求在属性或索引器中，`get`访问器必须位于`set`或`init`访问器之前。

## 规则意义
遵循这一规则可以提高代码的可读性和一致性，使开发人员能更快速准确地理解代码的意图，以及属性的访问顺序。

## 错误示例
```csharp
public string Name
{ 
    set { this.name = value; }
    get { return this.name; }
}
```

## 正确示例
```csharp
public string Name
{ 
    get { return this.name; }
    set { this.name = value; }
}
```

rules.content_en:
# Rule Overview
This rule requires that within a property or indexer, the `get` accessor must appear before the `set` or `init` accessor.

## Rule Meaning
Following this rule enhances code readability and consistency, allowing developers to quickly and accurately comprehend the code intent and the order of property access.

## Error Example
```csharp
public string Name
{ 
    set { this.name = value; }
    get { return this.name; }
}
```

## Correct Example
```csharp
public string Name
{ 
    get { return this.name; }
    set { this.name = value; }
}
```

rules.content:
# 规则概述
在C#中，事件的访问器包括`add`和`remove`。遵循代码风格的最佳实践，`add`访问器应该位于`remove`访问器之前。

## 规则意义
保持代码一致性和可读性，有助于开发者遵循一致的编程惯例，从而减少错误和混淆。

## 错误示例
```csharp
public event EventHandler NameChanged
{ 
    remove { this.nameChanged -= value; }
    add { this.nameChanged += value; }
}
```

## 正确示例
```csharp
public event EventHandler NameChanged
{ 
    add { this.nameChanged += value; }
    remove { this.nameChanged -= value; }
}
```

rules.content_en:
# Rule Overview
In C#, events have `add` and `remove` accessors. According to best code style practices, the `add` accessor should precede the `remove` accessor.

## Rule Meaning
Maintaining code consistency and readability helps developers follow a unified programming convention, thus reducing errors and confusion.

## Error Example
```csharp
public event EventHandler NameChanged
{ 
    remove { this.nameChanged -= value; }
    add { this.nameChanged += value; }
}
```

## Correct Example
```csharp
public event EventHandler NameChanged
{ 
    add { this.nameChanged += value; }
    remove { this.nameChanged -= value; }
}
```

rules.content:
# 规则概述
在 C# 中，所有的只读字段应位于非只读字段之前。这有助于增强代码的可读性和一致性。

## 规则意义
在代码中正确地排列字段有助于开发人员和代码审查者更容易理解代码结构。此外，它也遵循了代码书写的一致性规则，使代码更具可维护性和可读性。

## 错误示例
```csharp
public class ExampleClass
{
    private int nonReadonlyField;
    private readonly int readonlyField;
}
```

## 正确示例
```csharp
public class ExampleClass
{
    private readonly int readonlyField;
    private int nonReadonlyField;
}
```

rules.content_en:
# Rule Overview
In C#, all readonly fields should come before non-readonly fields. This helps enhance code readability and consistency.

## Rule Meaning
Correctly ordering fields in the code assists developers and code reviewers in better understanding the structure of the code. Moreover, it adheres to consistent coding rules, making the code more maintainable and readable.

## Error Example
```csharp
public class ExampleClass
{
    private int nonReadonlyField;
    private readonly int readonlyField;
}
```

## Correct Example
```csharp
public class ExampleClass
{
    private readonly int readonlyField;
    private int nonReadonlyField;
}
```

